 3. 💰 Scoop: AI agents slip into bank accounts 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images 

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Major U.S. banks are detecting unauthorized third-party AI agents operating inside customer accounts — with some agents attempting to take actions that appear to be independent of user instructions, sources tell Axios' Madison Mills. 

 Why it matters: AI agents are quickly becoming a nightmare for banks, slipping into their systems faster than they can respond. Because it's hard to distinguish a human from a bot, banks are blocking the technology and rapidly rewriting their terms of service.

 Morgan Stanley, Bank of America, JPMorgan and Wells Fargo are among the banks racing to contain agent risks. Some are aware of unauthorized access, people familiar with the issue said. 🔭 Zoom in: These are generally situations when an agent or AI assistant accesses a human's account with the user's permission and login credentials. 

 That violates guidelines of most banks, which require humans to log in to their own accounts. Banking execs fear what agents with login access might do, especially if a user has granted purchase authority or the ability to shift funds.

 Some banks have tracked activity when agents — programmed to fulfill requests or complete tasks on a user's behalf — contact call centers or appear to take actions independent of user instructions, the people said. Keep reading . 

 
 
